--TEST--
`as Core\Html\Markup` lifts a string literal into the carrier the HTML sink writes raw — ADR 0024 §§ 3 and 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5: a `Markup` is the sink's only raw-write bypass, and `as` on a string
// literal is the way one is obtained — the class is memberless, so there is no
// constructor to reach instead.
var $m = "<b>bold</b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
echo $m, "\n";

// The bound on the other side, over the identical text: § 3's launderer is
// what every non-`Markup` value goes through, and it writes the same
// characters as references. The two lines together are the whole of what the
// carrier buys — the same bytes, one path trusted and one escaped — so a lift
// that quietly escaped, or an `escape` that quietly stopped, fails here.
echo Core\Html::escape("<b>bold</b>"), "\n";

// All five characters survive the lift, none of them escaped, counted as one
// string rather than read off five lines.
echo "&<>\"'" as Core\Html\Markup, "\n";

// A parenthesized literal is exactly as trusted as a bare one: § 5's rule is
// about where the bytes came from, not about how the expression was written.
echo ("<hr>") as Core\Html\Markup, "\n";
--EXPECT--
<b>bold</b>
&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;
&<>"'
<hr>
